<p>Pakistan said it is preparing to host U.S.-Iran talks in the coming days, after the foreign ministers of Saudi Arabia, Turkey, and Egypt met in Islamabad and backed efforts to end the war over Iran. The diplomatic opening came as Tehran issued sharp public warnings that any U.S. ground operation would face fierce resistance, while President Trump said a deal could be reached soon even as he told the Financial Times he was considering options including taking Iran’s oil and potentially seizing Kharg Island, Iran’s main export hub. The mix of military threats and renewed mediation shows both how dangerous the moment is and how a path to de-escalation still exists. Multiple reports say the war has entered its second month, while Euronews said recent attacks spread beyond Iran to Gulf states including Kuwait and Saudi Arabia. Pakistan said it would be honored to facilitate meaningful talks, with support also reported from the United Nations and China, offering one of the clearest signs in these reports that regional and international actors still see diplomacy as the best way forward.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/UFwRT6cuAGi44DQj_fsiuNDt6XkTC2Xy7LHW_WzWBThp31eovwqomMiExFWuc-p_LU7Va3McJftl-BUwR2ji5PJM1yodvKfrEDEvU29b4A6z2AH_uA673RXjIuep792HED1fjRcs6rYMHs75-8vhNeKIz5u2n7n3SzFixopw0IHFIaaDPeiRdtjHFqkirydS5k_OurFpCYFSF10zd1M1Z-jJkwLldByGQrQ' alt='A motorcade arriving at the Ministry of Foreign Affairs office in Islamabad, Pakistan, on Sunday.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Regional backing: Pakistan’s foreign minister Ishaq Dar said Saudi Arabia, Turkey, and Egypt expressed “full support” for possible U.S.-Iran talks in Islamabad, expanding the mediation effort beyond bilateral contacts.</li><li>Kharg stakes: Taking Iran’s oil, as President Trump described it, would likely require invading and holding Kharg Island, a strategic export hub that analysts say is central to Iran’s crude shipments.</li><li>War duration: Iran’s parliament speaker issued his latest warning in a message marking 30 days since the war began, underscoring that the conflict has now stretched into a second month.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Pakistan: Islamabad cast itself as a mediator ready to host “meaningful talks” and said its diplomacy is aimed at securing an early and permanent end to the war. (<a href='https://www.al-monitor.com/originals/2026/03/pakistan-prepares-host-peace-talks-iran-accuses-us-ground-assault-plans'>Al-Monitor</a>)</li><li>Iran: Parliament speaker Mohammad Bagher Ghalibaf accused Washington of signaling negotiations in public while secretly planning a land attack, and warned that U.S. troops would face severe risks if they entered Iran. (<a href='https://www.cbsnews.com/news/iran-warns-us-against-ground-operation/'>CBS News</a>)</li><li>United States: President Trump said he is pretty sure a peace deal will be reached and described negotiations as going “extremely well,” while also keeping military and oil-seizure options on the table in public. (<a href='https://www.rferl.org/a/pakistan-iran-war-israel-marines-qalibaf/33719293.html'>RFE/RL</a>)</li><li>Regional ministers: The foreign ministers of Egypt, Pakistan, Turkey, and Saudi Arabia said they fully support potential Iran-United States talks hosted by Pakistan. (<a href='https://www.france24.com/en/live-news/20260329-pakistan-hosts-saudi-turkey-egypt-for-talks-on-mideast-war'>France 24</a>)</li><li>International bodies: Pakistan said its peace initiative has support from the United Nations and China, suggesting outside backing for a negotiated off-ramp. (<a href='https://www.al-monitor.com/originals/2026/03/pakistan-says-growing-support-its-mideast-war-talks-plan'>Al-Monitor</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.cbsnews.com/news/iran-warns-us-against-ground-operation/'>Iran warns troops will be &quot;set on fire&quot; if U.S. launches ground operation</a> - cbsnews.com</li><li><a href='https://www.ft.com/content/3bd9fb6c-2985-4d24-b8